Scenario 1: You remove @Component but define the bean manually

If you remove @Component from your LoggingAspect class and instead define it manually in a configuration class using @Bean, Spring AOP will still work perfectly.
• Why it works: Spring AOP does not explicitly require @Component. What it actually requires is that the aspect class is registered as a Spring Bean in the Application Context.
• The Manual Approach:java
@Configuration
public class AopConfig {
    @Bean
    public LoggingAspect loggingAspect() {
        return new LoggingAspect();
    }
}
Use code with caution.
• Behind the scenes: When Spring boots up, it scans the container for any registered bean that has the @Aspect annotation on it. Because your manually created bean still has @Aspect, Spring successfully flags it, processes its pointcuts, and wraps your target services in proxies.

Scenario 2: You remove @Aspect

If you remove @Aspect from the class (even if it is still annotated with @Component or created manually as a bean), Spring AOP will completely stop working for this class.
• Why it fails: The @Aspect annotation is the absolute trigger that tells Spring's auto-proxy creator (AnnotationAwareAspectJAutoProxyCreator) to process the class for AOP.
• What happens to the class: Spring will treat the class as a regular, boring Java bean.
• What happens to the advices (@Before, @Around, etc.): Spring will entirely ignore these annotations inside the class. They will just sit there as dead code.
• The Result: No proxies will be generated around your service methods, and your logging or security logic will never execute when business methods run.

Summary Checklist

Configuration	Registered as a Bean?	Has @Aspect?	Does AOP Work?
Standard @Component + @Aspect	Yes (Auto-scanned)	Yes	Yes
Manual @Bean + @Aspect	Yes (Manual)	Yes	Yes
@Component only (No @Aspect)	Yes	No	No (Treated as a regular bean)
@Aspect only (Not registered as a bean)	No	Yes	No (Spring doesn't know it exists)

